    labs: "Hb 7.2 g/dL (12-15). MCV 72 fL (80-100) — microcytic. MCH 24 pg (27-32). RDW 17% (elevated — anisocytosis). Ferritin 8 ng/mL (15-150). Serum Iron 30 mcg/dL (50-150). TIBC 480 mcg/dL (250-400) — elevated. Transferrin saturation 6% (15-50). Platelets 450 (reactive thrombocytosis). Hb electrophoresis: normal (rules out thalassaemia trait).",
    imaging: "No imaging indicated. Pelvic ultrasound if menorrhagia workup needed: normal uterine anatomy, no fibroids.",
    diagnosis: "Moderate-to-Severe Iron Deficiency Anaemia (Microcytic, Hypochromic) Secondary to Menorrhagia",
    ddx: ["Iron Deficiency Anaemia", "Thalassaemia Trait", "Anaemia of Chronic Disease", "Lead Poisoning", "Vitamin B6 Deficiency"],
    goals: "Correct anaemia, replenish iron stores, identify and treat underlying cause (menorrhagia), improve quality of life",
    pharm: "1. Oral iron replacement: Ferrous Sulphate 200mg (65mg elemental iron) TID taken between meals (or with Vitamin C to enhance absorption). 2. If intolerant: Ferrous Gluconate or Ferrous Fumarate. 3. Duration: 3-6 months (until Hb normalised AND ferritin >50 ng/mL). 4. Consider IV iron (Iron Sucrose or Ferric Carboxymaltose) if: severe anaemia with symptoms, intolerance to oral iron, non-adherence, or ongoing blood loss. 5. For menorrhagia management: Tranexamic acid 1g TID during menses. Hormonal therapy (combined OCP or Mirena IUS) if appropriate.",
    nonPharm: "Dietary counselling: increase heme iron sources (red meat, liver, fish) and enhance non-heme iron absorption with Vitamin C (citrus, tomatoes). Avoid tea/coffee with meals (tannins inhibit absorption).",
    carePlan: "Start oral ferrous sulphate 200mg TID. Advise about side effects (dark stools, constipation, nausea). Check Hb and ferritin in 4-6 weeks. If Hb rise >2 g/dL, continue until ferritin normalised. If poor response: check adherence, consider ongoing blood loss, check for malabsorption (coeliac).",
    dtps: "1. Iron deficiency from chronic blood loss exceeding dietary iron intake. 2. Pica (pagophagia) indicating moderate-to-severe deficiency.",
    monitoring: "Hb and reticulocyte count in 2 weeks (reticulocytosis indicates bone marrow response). Hb, MCV, ferritin at 4-6 weeks. Target: Hb rise >1 g/dL every 2-3 weeks. Continue until ferritin >50 ng/mL (usually 3-6 months total).",
    counselling: "Iron tablets cause dark stools (harmless), constipation, and sometimes nausea. Take them on an empty stomach with Vitamin C (orange juice). If stomach upset, take with a small amount of food. Do not take with tea, coffee, or milk. Iron poisoning is dangerous — keep tablets away from children.",
    followUp: "GP follow-up in 4-6 weeks for repeat Hb. Gynaecology referral for menorrhagia management if not already addressed. Once Hb normalised, continue iron for 3 more months to replenish stores.",
    pearls: "Iron deficiency is the most common deficiency worldwide. In premenopausal women, menorrhagia is the most common cause until proven otherwise. Ferritin is the best marker of iron stores — it is an acute phase reactant, so check CRP concurrently (ferritin can be falsely normal in inflammation). Pica (pagophagia = ice craving) is highly specific for iron deficiency.",
    references: ["WHO Guidelines: Iron Deficiency Anaemia Prevention", "BSH Guidelines: Iron Deficiency Anaemia", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Anaemia Management"],
