    carePlan: "Start IM Hydroxocobalamin loading. Add Folic acid 5mg daily. Monitor reticulocyte count (should rise in 3-5 days, peak at 7-10 days). Hb should increase by 1 g/dL per week. Neurological improvement is slower (weeks to months) and may be incomplete if damage is severe or prolonged.",
    dtps: "1. Severe B12 deficiency causing both megaloblastic anaemia and neurological damage. 2. Dietary deficiency due to strict vegetarian diet — risk of irreversible neurological damage if untreated.",
    monitoring: "Reticulocyte count on Day 3-5 (should show brisk response). Hb, MCV, WBC, platelets weekly until normalised. B12 levels monthly during loading phase. Neurological exam: vibration sense, gait, Romberg, reflexes. Improvement in neurological symptoms typically begins at 4-6 weeks.",
    counselling: "B12 is found only in animal-source foods. If you continue a vegetarian diet, you must take B12 supplements for life — the deficiency can cause permanent nerve damage and paralysis. Injections will quickly improve your energy, but nerve recovery may take months. Tingling should improve first; balance may take longer.",
    followUp: "Haematology/endocrine clinic in 4 weeks. Continue IM B12 monthly. Neurological assessment at 3 and 6 months. Repeat B12 and FBC at 3 months to confirm adequacy of maintenance regimen.",
    pearls: "Megaloblastic anaemia from B12 deficiency is characterised by macrocytic RBCs (MCV >100) and hypersegmented neutrophils (more specific than MCV alone). The classic triad: anaemia, glossitis, and neuropathy. Subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord affects: posterior columns (vibration/proprioception loss → sensory ataxia) + corticospinal tracts (spasticity, Babinski) + peripheral nerves. NEVER give folate without B12 if B12 deficiency is suspected — this corrects the anaemia but allows neurological damage to progress.",
    references: ["BSH Guidelines: B12 and Folate Deficiency", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Macrocytic Anaemia", "WHO: Micronutrient Supplementation"],
