    pearls: "Megaloblastic anaemia from B12 deficiency is characterised by macrocytic RBCs (MCV >100) and hypersegmented neutrophils (more specific than MCV alone). The classic triad: anaemia, glossitis, and neuropathy. Subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord affects: posterior columns (vibration/proprioception loss → sensory ataxia) + corticospinal tracts (spasticity, Babinski) + peripheral nerves. NEVER give folate without B12 if B12 deficiency is suspected — this corrects the anaemia but allows neurological damage to progress.",
